1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:04,560
At the end of the day, a merchant asks one question: how much do I get?

2
00:00:04,610 --> 00:00:12,576
Every night the bank adds up the day before, for each website: what was paid, the fee, what was refunded, and the payout.

3
00:00:12,626 --> 00:00:21,288
A business day runs from midnight to midnight in the settlement time zone, the same day that decides reversal or refund, so the two never disagree.

4
00:00:21,338 --> 00:00:28,512
Paid counts payments paid that day and not reversed; refunded counts refunds made that day, whenever the payment was.

5
00:00:28,562 --> 00:00:34,944
The payout is the net of what was paid, less what was refunded: the bank keeps its fee on a refund.

6
00:00:34,994 --> 00:00:39,264
Building a day again replaces its rows, and never adds to them.

7
00:00:39,314 --> 00:00:47,472
So every instance may run the job, and each night it rebuilds the last three days: a night the app was down is caught up.

8
00:00:47,522 --> 00:00:56,184
On the scheme's side, Humo finalises an e POS terminal's payments, and pays its returns back, only after the terminal is reconciled.

9
00:00:56,234 --> 00:01:10,080
So the card module reconciles every Humo e POS terminal fifteen minutes before each clearing session, and once between eleven forty-five and midnight; a unique row per terminal and slot lets only the first instance run it.

10
00:01:10,130 --> 00:01:19,056
Behind each day is a CSV: every payment and refund, with its fee, card and RRN, and a refund as a negative line.

11
00:01:19,106 --> 00:01:23,280
Every cell is quoted, because a website's name may hold a comma.

12
00:01:23,330 --> 00:01:31,968
In the portal, the merchant sees each day and downloads its CSV; a day where refunds outweigh payments shows a negative payout.

13
00:01:32,018 --> 00:01:35,832
Next, hardening: limits on what one website may take.
